description:
FOUND BY AN EXISTING TEST, FIX IS IN THE WORKING TREE, NOT YET COMMITTED (CrimsonBeaver, 2026-08-14).

SYMPTOM: tests/python/test_view_descriptor_parity.py::test_direct_edge_attribute_self_reference_is_stored_and_collectable[Graph] FAILED on a build carrying the in-flight edge_py_attrs_by_endpoint lookaside cache. Full suite on that build: 1 failed / 55104 passed. The same test passed on the immediately preceding build (55097 passed / 0 failed), which had every other change but not the cache.

CAUSE, read from source and confirmed by the fix: crates/fnx-python/src/lib.rs. The new cache is a nested HashMap<String, HashMap<String, Py<PyDict>>> holding a SECOND strong reference to every edge's attribute dict (materialize_edge_py_attrs inserts attrs.clone_ref(py) into it). clear_python_refs DOES clear it. traverse_python_refs did NOT visit it.

CPython requires tp_traverse and tp_clear to cover the same references. A reference that is cleared but never traversed is invisible to the cycle collector, so a cycle running through it is never detected and the objects leak. An edge attr dict that references itself is exactly such a cycle, which is why that specific test caught it and nothing else did.

FIX (in the working tree, uncommitted, in traverse_python_refs immediately after the edge_py_attrs loop):

    for row in self.edge_py_attrs_by_endpoint.values() {
        for attrs in row.values() {
            visit.call(attrs)?;
        }
    }

VERIFIED: with the fix, tests/python/test_view_descriptor_parity.py + tests/python/test_view_borrowed_canonical_key_parity.py = 340 passed, including the previously failing case.

WHY IT IS NOT COMMITTED BY ME: the fix lives in lib.rs alongside the cache's own field declaration and lookup logic, which are another agent's in-flight work. Committing the file would put their unfinished design in history under my name. Whoever owns edge_py_attrs_by_endpoint should commit the cache, its 25 initializers (also completed in the working tree) and this traverse fix as ONE unit. If the cache is abandoned instead, this bead closes with it.

GENERAL RULE WORTH KEEPING: any new field on PyGraph/PyDiGraph/PyMultiGraph/PyMultiDiGraph that holds a Py<...> must be added to traverse_python_refs and clear_python_refs TOGETHER. The asymmetry is silent — it costs nothing at the call site and only shows up as a leak under a cycle. Related to the same-shaped requirement noted on br-r37-c1-5gam7 for view GC.

CLOSING PROBE: the named test above must pass on a build containing the cache.

comments.text:
ANSWER TO THE QUESTION THIS BEAD WAS NARROWED TO — AND IT DISCONFIRMS MY OWN CANDIDATE (OliveDesert, 2026-08-08). Read from source, consistent with the measurement, NOT yet attributed by profiling. Bead stays open.

THE QUESTION: what does a native add_node that changes nothing spend 538 ns on? Measured last comment: no-op 538.2 ns, real insert 596.2 ns, so the insert is 10% and ~90% runs regardless.

MY CANDIDATE WAS THE CGSE DECISION LEDGER, on the grounds that g2nev showed its cost is retention per CALL rather than per mutation, which matches the shape of a costly no-op. Reading PyGraph::add_node (crates/fnx-python/src/lib.rs:12406 — confirmed impl PyGraph, not the PyMultiGraph one at :6740) there is NO ledger call in the function at all. The candidate is disconfirmed for this path.

WHAT THE FUNCTION ACTUALLY DOES ON A NO-OP, in order:

    let canonical = node_key_to_string(py, node_for_adding)?;   // owned String
    let was_new = !self.inner.has_node(&canonical);
    if self.should_store_node_key(&canonical, was_new) {
        self.node_key_map.entry(canonical.clone())              // clone 2
            .or_insert_with(|| node_for_adding.clone().unbind());
    }
    let py_dict = self.node_py_attrs.entry(canonical.clone())   // clone 3
        .or_insert_with(|| PyDict::new(py).unbind());
    ...
    self.inner.add_node_with_attrs(canonical.clone(), rust_attrs);  // clone 4
    log::debug!(target: "franken_networkx", "add_node: {canonical}");
    if was_new { self.node_iter_mirror_insert(py, &canonical)?; }
    self.bump_nodes_seq();

FOUR heap allocations of the SAME canonical key — one owned from node_key_to_string plus three explicit .clone() calls — and three separate map probes that each hash it, on a call that mutates nothing. The `entry(canonical.clone())` pattern allocates the key eagerly whether or not the entry is vacant, which for an existing node is pure waste in both of the two places it appears. `add_node_with_attrs` is also called unconditionally for a node already present.

WHY THIS IS CONSISTENT WITH THE NUMBERS: it is per-CALL, not per-mutation, which is the shape the 538 ns no-op demands. It is also consistent with key type being free on this path (measured two comments ago, 532.9 str against 539.7 int) — the cost is allocating and hashing the key repeatedly, not deriving it, and both key types allocate the same way.

STATUS, STATED CAREFULLY BECAUSE I HAVE MIS-ATTRIBUTED TWICE ON THIS BEAD ALREADY: this is a reading of the source that fits every measurement taken, not a profile. Four allocations plus three hashes of a short String is plausibly a few hundred nanoseconds, but I have not timed the individual clones and I am not claiming a number for them. The in-crate self-time profile should start here — time the function with the three .clone() calls replaced by borrows or by a single reused key — and it is a cheap first experiment because the change is local.

IF IT HOLDS, the lever is ordinary: compute the canonical key once and stop cloning it. Both `entry(canonical.clone())` sites can use the raw-entry or get-then-insert shape that only allocates on the vacant path, and add_node_with_attrs can take the key by value exactly once at the end. That is a same-shape change to the one already shipped four times in the batch collectors (br-r37-c1-uta2n and siblings), where the fix was likewise to stop paying an allocation the common path does not need.

CGSE LEDGER STATUS: still relevant to add_edge_with_attrs, where g2nev measured it, but not visible on add_node. Do not carry my earlier candidate across.

description:
fnx.hits() returns values that don't match nx.hits(). The hub and authority scores differ significantly:

Test graph: DiGraph with edges [(0, 1), (0, 2), (1, 2), (2, 0), (2, 3), (3, 0)]

FNX HITS:
  Hubs: {0: 3.01, 1: 1.86, 2: -2.39, 3: -1.48}
  Authorities: {0: -2.39, 1: 1.86, 2: 3.01, 3: -1.48}

NX HITS:
  Hubs: {0: -0.18, 1: -0.11, 2: 0.79, 3: 0.49}
  Authorities: {0: 0.79, 1: -0.11, 2: -0.18, 3: 0.49}

The fnx values appear to be from a different eigenvector or incorrectly normalized. Both sum to ~1.0 but the actual values are very different.

## What

Add doc comments to the four MAX_N_* constants in fnx-generators explaining WHY
each specific value was chosen and what would break if they were changed.

## Current Constants (fnx-generators/src/lib.rs lines 13-16)

- MAX_N_GENERIC: 100_000 — general graph generation cap
- MAX_N_STAR: 99_999 (MAX_N_GENERIC - 1) — star graph hub has n edges
- MAX_N_COMPLETE: 2_000 — K_n has n*(n-1)/2 edges; K_2000 = ~2M edges
- MAX_N_GNP: 20_000 — G(n,p) is O(n²); n=20000 → ~200M edge candidates

## Why This Matters

These bounds exist to prevent denial-of-service via adversarial graph generation
(Security Doctrine in AGENTS.md). But they're currently undocumented, which means:
1. A contributor might raise them without understanding memory implications
2. They don't explain the relationship between n and memory (quadratic for complete/GNP)
3. The hardened vs strict mode implications aren't captured

## What To Do

Add comments like:
```rust
/// Maximum nodes for generic generators. Chosen to keep memory under ~1GB
/// for adjacency-list representation with average degree ~10.
const MAX_N_GENERIC: usize = 100_000;

/// Star graph cap. K_{1,n} has exactly n edges, so MAX_N_STAR = MAX_N_GENERIC - 1
/// avoids exceeding the generic edge budget when the hub connects to all others.
const MAX_N_STAR: usize = MAX_N_GENERIC - 1;

/// Complete graph cap. K_n has n*(n-1)/2 edges. K_2000 ≈ 2M edges ≈ 48MB for
/// edge storage alone. Beyond this, memory consumption becomes problematic.
const MAX_N_COMPLETE: usize = 2_000;

/// G(n,p) cap. The naive O(n²) algorithm iterates all n*(n-1)/2 possible edges.
/// At n=20000, that's ~200M iterations — acceptable on modern hardware but any
/// higher risks multi-second generation times even in Rust.
const MAX_N_GNP: usize = 20_000;
```

## Files To Change

- crates/fnx-generators/src/lib.rs (lines 13-16)

## Verification

- cargo check -p fnx-generators (doc comments don't affect compilation)
- No test changes needed

fnx.write_gml(Graph()) produces "graph [
  directed 0
]
" but nx.write_gml produces "graph [
]
" (omits the directed field for undirected graphs). Both are readable by nx.read_gml, but the byte-level divergence surprises users comparing outputs. Low priority.
